### Step 1. Create three Ubuntu instances

Create:

- kube-master
- kube-worker1
- kube-worker2

Connect to all three machines through SSH.

### Step 2. Configure all three machines

Run these commands on every machine.
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y

## Set hostnames ##

On Master:
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname kube-master

On Worker 1:
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname kube-worker1

On Worker 2:
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname kube-worker2
Reconnect to the machines.

Verify:
hostname

### Step 3. Configure hosts file

On all three machines:
sudo nano /etc/hosts

Add the actual private IP addresses:
172.31.10.10 kube-master
172.31.10.11 kube-worker1
172.31.10.12 kube-worker2
Replace these sample IPs with your actual private IPs.

Verify:
ping -c 3 kube-master
ping -c 3 kube-worker1
ping -c 3 kube-worker2

### Step 4. Disable swap

Explain first:
What is Swap?
Swap is disk space used as an extension of RAM.
For our standard Kubeadm configuration, we will disable swap.
Run on all three machines:
sudo swapoff -a

Make it persistent:
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab

Verify:
free -h

### Step 5. Configure kernel modules

Run on all three machines:
sudo modprobe overlay
sudo modprobe br_netfilter

Make the configuration persistent:

sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf <<EOF
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF

Configure networking:

sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf <<EOF
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables = 1
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
EOF

Apply:
sudo sysctl --system

Verify:
lsmod | grep br_netfilter

And:
sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward

Expected:
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1

### Step 6. Install containerd
Run on all three machines:

sudo apt install -y containerd

Create configuration directory:
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd

Generate default configuration:
sudo containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml

Now configure SystemdCgroup.
Open:
sudo nano /etc/containerd/config.toml

Find:
SystemdCgroup = false

Change it to:
SystemdCgroup = true

Also ensure the CRI plugin is not disabled in the configuration.

Restart containerd:
sudo systemctl restart containerd

Enable containerd:
sudo systemctl enable containerd

Verify:
sudo systemctl status containerd

## Why SystemdCgroup?
Linux uses control groups (cgroups) to manage resource allocation.
Kubernetes and the container runtime should use compatible cgroup management.
Using the systemd cgroup driver.

# PART 7 – INSTALL KUBEADM, KUBELET AND KUBECTL
This step is performed on all three machines.

### Step 7. Install required packages
sudo apt install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl gpg

Create keyring directory:
sudo mkdir -p -m 755 /etc/apt/keyrings

Download the Kubernetes signing key:
curl -fsSL https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.37/deb/Release.key | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg

Add Kubernetes repository:
echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg] https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.37/deb/ /' | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list

Update:
sudo apt update

Install:
sudo apt install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl

Hold packages:
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl

Enable kubelet:
sudo systemctl enable kubelet

Check versions:
kubeadm version
kubectl version --client
kubelet --version
Important: kubelet may repeatedly restart before the cluster is initialized. This is expected in a fresh Kubeadm setup.

# PART 8 – INITIALIZE THE CONTROL PLANE
Now we will work only on the Master machine.

### Step 8. Initialize the cluster
First, identify the private IP:
hostname -I

Suppose the private IP is:
172.31.10.10
Run:
sudo kubeadm init --apiserver-advertise-address=172.31.10.10 --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16

Replace the IP with your Master private IP.

### What happens internally when we execute kubeadm init?
Kubeadm performs several operations:

1. Performs preflight checks.
2. Generates Kubernetes certificates.
3. Generates kubeconfig files.
4. Creates etcd static Pod manifest.
5. Creates API Server static Pod manifest.
6. Creates Controller Manager static Pod manifest.
7. Creates Scheduler static Pod manifest.
8. Configures kubelet.
9. Starts the Control Plane components.
10. Creates bootstrap tokens.
11. Configures CoreDNS.
12. Configures kube-proxy.

Note: Control Plane components are initially deployed as Static Pods.

Kubelet watches:
/etc/kubernetes/manifests/

### Step 9. Configure kubectl

After successful initialization, run:
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube

Copy the admin configuration:
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config

Change ownership:
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config

Verify:
kubectl get nodes

Initially, the Master may show:
NAME          STATUS     ROLES           AGE
kube-master   NotReady   control-plane   2m

## Why is the Master NotReady?
Because we have not installed the CNI network plugin yet.
This is an important conceptual connection.

# PART 9 – INSTALL CNI NETWORKING
## 8. What is CNI?
CNI stands for Container Network Interface. It provides the networking infrastructure required for Pods to communicate. Without a functioning CNI plugin, Pods will not have the required network connectivity.

There are several CNI solutions:

| CNI | Description |
| Flannel | Simple Pod networking |
| Calico | Networking and network policies |
| Cilium | eBPF-based networking and security |
| Weave Net | Legacy networking solution |

We will use Flannel because it is relatively straightforward.

### Step 10. Install Flannel
We initialized our cluster with:
--pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16
Flannel's default network configuration uses the same CIDR.

Run on Master:
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml
Check:
kubectl get pods -n kube-flannel

Check nodes:
kubectl get nodes
Expected:
NAME          STATUS   ROLES           AGE
kube-master   Ready    control-plane   10m

The Master should now become Ready once networking is functioning.

# PART 10 – JOIN WORKER NODES
This is where students understand the real meaning of `kubeadm join`.

## 9. What is kubeadm join?
`kubeadm join` is used to register another machine with an existing Kubernetes cluster.

We have initialized our Master.
Now we need to add Worker nodes.

### Step 11. Generate the join command
Run on the master:
kubeadm token create --print-join-command

It generates a command similar to:
sudo kubeadm join 172.31.10.10:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:xxxxxxxx

Our actual token and certificate hash will be different.
Copy the complete command.

### Step 12. Join Worker 1
Connect to Worker 1.
Execute the generated command:

sudo kubeadm join 172.31.10.10:6443 --token YOUR_TOKEN --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:YOUR_HASH

### Step 13. Join Worker 2
Connect to Worker 2.
Execute the same generated join command, provided the token is still valid.

### Step 14. Verify the cluster
Go back to Master:
kubectl get nodes
Expected:
NAME          STATUS   ROLES           AGE
kube-master   Ready    control-plane   20m
kube-worker1  Ready    <none>          5m
kube-worker2  Ready    <none>          3m

Explain:

- Master manages the cluster.
- Worker nodes run applications.
- kubelet runs on every node.
- containerd runs containers.
- CNI provides Pod networking.

Now, We have successfully created a multi-node Kubernetes cluster using Kubeadm.
