We may need to configure crictl's runtime endpoint if it cannot automatically identify containerd.
Step 1. Understand the concept
crictl stands for Container Runtime Interface CLI.

It is a command-line utility used to inspect and troubleshoot containers and Pods managed by a Kubernetes-compatible container runtime.

Our architecture:
        Kubernetes
             |
          kubelet
             |
       CRI Interface
             |
         containerd
             |
       Linux Containers

crictl allows us to inspect the runtime directly.
Step 2. Identify the containerd socket
On each machine, execute:
sudo ls -l /run/containerd/containerd.sock

Expected:
/run/containerd/containerd.sock

This is the Unix socket through which clients communicate with containerd.

Step 3. Configure crictl
Execute:
sudo crictl config --set runtime-endpoint=unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock --set image-endpoint=unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock

This creates or updates:
/etc/crictl.yaml

Step 4. Verify configuration
cat /etc/crictl.yaml

Expected:
runtime-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
image-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
timeout: 2
debug: false

Step 5. Test containerd connectivity
Execute:
sudo crictl info

If everything is configured correctly, you should receive runtime information in JSON format.
Step 6. Check containers
sudo crictl ps

To see all containers, including stopped containers:
sudo crictl ps -a

Step 7. Check downloaded images
sudo crictl images

Step 8. Check the containerd service
sudo systemctl status containerd

If it is not running:
sudo systemctl restart containerd

