# PART 13 – IMPORTANT KUBEADM ADMINISTRATION COMMANDS

Do not stop after cluster creation.

## 10. Node management
## Check nodes
kubectl get nodes

## Describe a node
kubectl describe node kube-worker1

## Mark a node unschedulable
kubectl cordon kube-worker1

## Drain a node
kubectl drain kube-worker1 --ignore-daemonsets

Note: This may require additional flags depending on local storage and workload configuration. It also evicts eligible Pods.

## Allow scheduling again
kubectl uncordon kube-worker1

Explain the difference:
cordon ----  Prevent new Pods from being scheduled 
 drain --- Evict eligible existing Pods and mark node unschedulable 
 uncordon --- Allow scheduling again 

## 11. Bootstrap token management

Generate token:
sudo kubeadm token create

List tokens:
sudo kubeadm token list

Generate complete join command:
sudo kubeadm token create --print-join-command

## 12. Certificate management
Kubernetes uses certificates for secure communication between components.

Check certificate expiry:
sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration

Check certificate directory:
sudo ls /etc/kubernetes/pki/

Important certificates include:
- ca.crt
- apiserver.crt
- apiserver-kubelet-client.crt
- front-proxy-ca.crt
- etcd/ca.crt

Ewhy certificates matter:

They provide authentication and secure communication between Kubernetes components.

