# PART 17 – KUBEADM RESET AND CLUSTER DELETION
How to remove a Kubeadm configuration. This is useful when a cluster initialization fails or when we want to repeat the practical.

### Step 1. Remove Worker nodes from Master
On Master:
kubectl drain kube-worker1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
kubectl delete node kube-worker1

Repeat for Worker 2.
### Step 2. Reset Worker 1

On Worker 1:
sudo kubeadm reset -f

### Step 3. Reset Worker 2
On Worker 2:
sudo kubeadm reset -f

### Step 4. Reset Master

On Master:
sudo kubeadm reset -f

### Step 5. Clean up CNI configuration

On all machines:
sudo rm -rf /etc/cni/net.d

Depending on your CNI and runtime configuration, additional cleanup may be required.
### Step 6. Remove kubeconfig
On Master:
rm -rf $HOME/.kube

# PART 18 – ADVANCED KUBEADM CONCEPTS
## 13. Single Control Plane vs High Availability
### Single Control Plane Architecture

          CONTROL PLANE
                |
       -------------------
       |                 |
    Worker 1          Worker 2

If the Control Plane fails, cluster management becomes unavailable. Existing workloads may continue running, depending on the failure.

### High Availability Architecture
A highly available cluster typically contains:
- Multiple Control Plane nodes.
- A load balancer for the Kubernetes API.
- Multiple Worker nodes.
- Highly available etcd.

Two major architectures:
##A. Stacked etcd topology
Each Control Plane node also runs an etcd member.

##B. External etcd topology
etcd runs on separate dedicated machines.

## 14. Kubeadm Upgrade
Kubernetes clusters need regular upgrades.
Explain the typical upgrade sequence:

1. Check the current Kubernetes version.
2. Review version-skew policies.
3. Back up etcd.
4. Upgrade the first Control Plane node.
5. Upgrade additional Control Plane nodes, if applicable.
6. Upgrade Worker nodes.
7. Verify cluster health.

